A||N||4||87ECO5||Problems with Brake System on my Nissan Pathfinder||||5||
Z||000000||87ECO5||03-27-2001||11:15 AM||||I've got a 1994 Nissan Pathfinder with 100,000 miles on it.  A few weeks ago, I started noticing a problem with the brake system.  The pedal doesn't feel overly spongy and the truck will stop quickly - <B>but only if you depress EXTREMELY hard on the pedal.</B><P>My guess is the power brake booster but how do I know for sure?  Is there a test that can be done on the booster to see if it's functioning properly?  Does my description of the problem sound like a booster failure to any of you?  A remanufactured PBB is $200-$300 so I'd kinda like to be sure before I just threw that money away.  I mean, $200-$300 would take care of the brake problem on my Fiero ... but that's another story altogether!<P>Any suggestions/help is GREATLY appreciated.<P>TIA||||reg||5
Z||000001||fiero56||03-27-2001||11:46 AM||||After you shut off the engine, wait a few minutes. Then, with the engine off, push the brake pedal down sorta' fast, if you don't hear that "whoosh" sound and the pedal doesn't move very far, you've got a leak somewhere. I don't know if all cars make that sound, but every car I've ever been in did. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||rogergarrison||03-27-2001||03:03 PM||||u can remove and plug the vacum line to booster and try brakes, if it feels the same (hard pedal pressure) boosters not working.||||reg||1
Z||000003||87ECO5||03-28-2001||06:21 AM||||(though it seems backwards to me) I tried the pedal test that Drew suggested.  I drove the truck to work, shut it off, waited a few seconds, then pressed rather quickly on the pedal.  The pedal traveled only a few inches and I got that "whooshing" sound.  I tried it again a few seconds later.  Same results only the pedal traveled less.  By the third attempt, the pedal wouldn't budge.<P>I located where the vacuum line connects to the booster.  As Roger suggested, I'll disconnect it and see if there is any change in the brake function.  Question:  once the line is removed, do I plug the line or the port where the line connects to the booster?||||reg||1
Z||000004||fiero56||03-28-2001||08:48 AM||||Pllug the line leading to the booster. 2 to 3 attempts should kill off most all vacuum, that sounds normal. Try it after its been sitting overnight too.||||reg||1
